'''baslard''' (bas′lärd), ''n.'' [< ME. ''baselard'', ''baslard'', ''baselarde'', < AF. ''baselard'', < OF. ''basalart'' (ML. ''bassilardus'', ''basalardus''), appar. < ''base'', a short knife or saber; but cf. OF. ''baselaire'', ''bazelaire'', ''badelaire'', a short sword: see ''badelaire''.] An ornamental dagger worn in the fifteenth century, hanging at the girdle in front. {{smaller|Baslards were considered indispensable to all having claim to gentility. In a satirical song of the reign of Henry V. we are told that}}
{{smaller block|<poem>There is no man worth a leke,
Be he sturdy, be he meke,
But he bere a ''baselard''.</poem>}}

